{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>These are the appointed seasons of YHWH.</b> He now begins the ordering of the appointed times and ascribes them to the proper Name. And he uses regarding them the word אלה ('these'), for the word אלה indicates permanence; it comes to hint that the appointed times are never abolished, but they remain in force as long as this physical world continues in its present order, as it is written (Deuteronomy 29:28): ״לנו ולבנינו עד עולם״. And so they said in the Midrash: a certain nation asked Rabbi Akiva, saying to him, 'Why do you observe festivals? Was it not said to you (Isaiah 1:14), ״חדשיכם ומועדיכם שנאה נפשי״?' He said to him, 'If it had been written חדש ומועד שנאה נפשי, I would say so; but it was not written חדש ומועד, but חדשיכם ומועדיכם — on account of those festivals that Jeroboam made, as it is written (1 Kings 12:33): ״ויעש ירבעם חג בחדש השמיני בחדש אשר בדא מלבו״, apart from what is written, that is (Leviticus 23), מלבד שבתות ה׳, and it is written (1 Kings 12:33): ״ויעל על המזבח אשר עשה בבית אל ויעש חג לפני ישראל״. But these appointed times are never abolished, for they are called מועדי ה׳, as it is written: אלה מועדי ה׳, and it is written: וידבר משה את מועדי ה׳, and regarding them it is said (Psalms 111:8): ״סמוכים לעד לעולם עשוים באמת וישר״.'" "<b>Which you shall proclaim.</b> It is written אתם ('you'), and they expounded it: אשר תקראו אתם — 'you shall proclaim them' — from here the sanctification of the festivals is given over to the court (bet din), even out of their proper time and even in error. And this is what they expounded, of blessed memory: אתם — even unintentionally; אתם — even mistakenly. And so Scripture says (Exodus 12:2): ״החדש הזה לכם״ — that is, it is yours." "<b>And so</b> you find in Pirkei Rabbi Eliezer: on the twenty-fifth of Elul the sun and moon were created. And the days and nights, seasons and intercalations were before the Holy One, blessed be He, and He would intercalate the year, and afterwards He handed them over to the first man, as it is written (Genesis 5:22): ״זה ספר תולדות אדם״. And Adam handed them over to Enoch, as it is written (ibid.): ״ויתהלך חנוך את האלהים״, and Enoch to Shem, and Shem to Abraham, and Abraham to Isaac, and Isaac to Jacob, until they came to Egypt, as it is written (Exodus 12:1): ״בארץ מצרים לאמר״. What is לאמר? The Holy One, blessed be He, said: Go, say to them: Until now the secret of intercalation was with Me; from now on it is yours, to intercalate the year. And so Israel would intercalate the year in the Land; when they were exiled to Babylon, they would intercalate it through those who remained in the Land. Ezekiel sought to intercalate the years in Babylon; the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: You have no permission to intercalate the year outside the Land — Israel who are in the Land shall intercalate the year. From here they said: Even righteous men and sages outside the Land, and cattle-herders in the Land — one does not intercalate the year except through the ordinary folk who are in the Land. And how did they intercalate the year? They would bring a Torah scroll before them and they would form a circular threshing floor, and the eldest would sit according to his seniority and the youngest according to his juniority. And they would turn their faces downward to the earth and stand and spread their hands upward to their Father in heaven, and the head of the academy would mention the Name, and they would hear a heavenly echo, in the manner of ״וידבר ה׳ אל משה לאמר״. And if the sin of the generation causes it, they hear nothing at all. And happy are those who stand in that place at that hour, as it is written (Psalms 89): ״אשרי העם יודעי תרועה ה׳ באור פניך יהלכון״ — in the light of His face, of the Holy One, blessed be He, they walk. Until here." "<b>And from here</b> one may learn the preeminence of the Holy Land and how beloved it is before the Place (HaMakom). And so they said in the Jerusalem Talmud, in the first chapter of Sanhedrin (Jeremiah 29:1): ״אל יתר זקני הגולה״ — the Holy One, blessed be He, said: 'The elders of the exile are upon Me' — a small group in the Land of Israel is more beloved to Me than a Great Sanhedrin outside the Land. And when the elders would gather in the Chamber of Hewn Stone in the Temple and they would say 'sanctified, sanctified' after receiving the testimony of the witnesses, that day would be fixed in heaven by the Great Sanhedrin on high to sit in judgment to judge the world on Rosh HaShanah. And the Holy One, blessed be He, and the Sanhedrin on high would concur through them and complete their verdict, and regarding this Scripture says (Psalms 57:3): ״אקרא לאלהים עליון לאל גומר עלי״ — that is, He would agree and conclude with them." "<b>And so</b> they said in the Midrash Tehillim (Deuteronomy 4:7): ״כה׳ אלהינו בכל קראנו אליו״. Rabbi Oshaya said: There is no nation that holds back its god like this nation. How so? At the hour when the elders sit to intercalate the year, the Holy One, blessed be He, concurs with them and He completes, etc., everything that Israel do. And regarding them David, peace be upon him, said: ״אקרא לאלהים עליון לאל גומר עלי״. When? On Rosh HaShanah, when the Sanhedrin sit to establish Rosh HaShanah on a certain day — immediately the Holy One, blessed be He, seats a Sanhedrin of angels and says to them: Go and conclude what the ones below have decreed. They say before Him: Master of the universe, Your sons have decreed that Rosh HaShanah be on such-and-such a day. Immediately the Holy One, blessed be He, sits on that day to judge His world, as it is said (Psalms 47:6): ״עלה אלהים בתרועה״, and the thrones are set out and the books are opened and the Sanhedrin of angels stand before Him, as it is written (Daniel 7:9): ״חזה הוית עד די כרסון רמיו ועתיק יומין יתיב״. These plead merit and those plead liability; why? (Psalms 81) ״כי חק לישראל הוא״ — Israel decreed the statute of Rosh HaShanah, and ״הוא משפט לאלהי יעקב״ — it is a judgment for the God of Jacob, for He upholds their decrees and concurs with them. Hence you say: ״אקרא לאלהים עליון לאל גומר עלי״. Know that this is so, for it is written (Numbers 29:1): ״יום תרועה יהיה לכם״ — לה׳ it does not say, but לכם. And it is written: אלה מועדי ה׳, and then אלה הם מועדי — the Holy One, blessed be He, said: I have no appointed times but yours, for all the appointed times that we sanctify are His, as it is written (Deuteronomy 4:7): ״כה׳ אלהינו בכל קראנו אליו״, and קראנו is none other than the sanctification of the festivals, as it is said: מקראי קדש. Hence you say: ״כה׳ אלהינו בכל קראנו אליו״. Until here in the Midrash."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :register {:flags {4 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:28:06.523502013Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}